+-- Expand: the size of what a tenant sent, recorded rather than recomputed
+-- (`relay/2.23-online-schema`).
+--
+-- Three properties make this migration shippable while the *previous* release is
+-- still running against the same table:
+--
+--   * nullable, and no default: `alter table … add column` with neither rewrites
+--     nothing and takes no lock worth waiting for, and an `insert` that names only
+--     the columns of `0003_events.sql` — which is what the old binary is doing
+--     right now — keeps working because the new column may be absent;
+--   * additive: no column is renamed, dropped or reinterpreted, so every query the
+--     old binary runs still means what it meant;
+--   * **nothing is backfilled here.** A migration that updates every row is a
+--     migration that holds a write lock over the whole table for as long as the
+--     update takes, on a table the old release is still writing to. The backfill
+--     is `src/backfill.rs`, run in batches, by whoever is deploying, and it can be
+--     stopped and run again because it only ever touches rows that have no value.
+--
+-- The contract phase — `not null`, and the read path in `src/pagination.rs` losing
+-- its fallback — is a *later release*, after no row can be null. Doing both halves
+-- in one deploy is what turns a rollout into an outage.
+alter table events
+    add column payload_bytes integer;
+
+-- The backfill asks for the rows that have no value yet, oldest first, and stops
+-- after its batch: an index in exactly that shape keeps it from reading the whole
+-- tenant history to find the handful of rows it still owes. Partial, because the
+-- rows with a value are not the question.
+create index events_missing_bytes_idx
+    on events (received_at, id)
+    where payload_bytes is null;
